  [1mWhat I left out, on purpose.[0m No [38;5;153mnotes[39m, no [38;5;153mdueDate[39m, no [38;5;153mpriority[39m, no tags, no assignee, no subtasks. Every one of those
  is easy to add later through the same funnel, and none of them teaches you anything about whether the architecture is
  right. Adding fields is cheap; unwinding a wrong change model is not. If you want any of them in slice 1, say so now —
  I'd rather add it deliberately than have it appear during implementation.

  The one omission I want to flag specifically: [1mno [38;5;153massigneeId[39m.[0m It's tempting because "tasks they care about" is
  eventually a subscription question and assignment is the obvious first answer. But with one user it would be a field
  that always equals [38;5;153mownerId[39m, and I'd be guessing at layer 3's design from inside layer 1. Better to add it when we
  design subscriptions and actually know what "care about" means — it might turn out to be watchers, or project
  membership, not assignment.

  [1mThe one real decision here: event granularity.[0m If you edit a task's title and status in a single save, is that one
  event or two?

  - [3mPer-field events[0m — two events, each atomic and precisely describable. Finer data, but a later notification layer has
    to group them back together or it sends you two notifications for one edit.
  - [3mPer-operation events with a [38;5;153mchanges[39m array[0m — one event that says "Alice edited this task, here's what moved." Matches
    how a person thinks about an action, and matches what a notification should say.

  I've written the schema above as [1mper-operation[0m, which is my recommendation: the event should correspond to the thing a
  human did, because that's the unit a notification will eventually describe. The [38;5;153mchanges[39m array keeps all the
  field-level detail, so nothing is lost — you can always fan one event out into per-field detail, whereas grouping
  per-field events back into an action requires guessing at timestamps.
